USER ## How the schemas are made — derived, never hand-edited
USER 
USER Plumbline no longer authors its own field names. It shipped a second job-packet schema that disagreed with
USER the corpus's own (measured: the corpus's fixtures give 18 errors against it). Two live schemas for one law
USER means neither is authority, and a hand-copied schema drifts silently.
USER 
USER ```
USER tools/build_schemas.py   corpus @ pin  +  schemas/instance-extensions.json
USER                          -> schemas/job-packet.schema.json       (corpus §3/§6 + instance layer)
USER                          -> schemas/evaluator-record.schema.json (corpus §5, shape-for-shape)
USER                          -> schemas/audit-record.schema.json     (corpus §5 + required audit extensions)
USER                          -> schemas/compliance-checklist.json    (Contracts §9, `why` re-attached by id)
USER                          -> tools/pin.json                       (commit + every source file's sha256)
USER ```
USER 
USER - **`schemas/instance-extensions.json` is the only place instance law is written.** Everything else in
USER   `schemas/` is derived and must never be edited by hand — a hand edit is erased by the next build and is
USER   invisible in review.
USER - **The build refuses to run when the corpus has moved.** Re-read the changed doctrine, then `--repin`.
USER   `--check` verifies the pin without writing; run `tests/run_all.py` before every commit (CI runs it too,
USER   with no corpus).
USER - **The corpus is vendored at the pin.** `schemas/vendor/e-di@f8bfb793/` holds the corpus's two schemas and
USER   its fixture battery, byte for byte, with `VENDOR.json` (provenance, every sha256). The battery runs on it, so
USER   the suites need no corpus; `tools/build_schemas.py --check` fails when the copy, `VENDOR.json` and
USER   `tools/pin.json` disagree. Never edit the copy; re-vendor it from the corpus when the pin moves.
USER - **Corpus resolution follows the edi-loop plugin's own rule** — `--corpus`, else `EDI_CORPUS`, else (under
USER   Hermes, in `tools/build_schemas.py` and `kit/plumbline.py`) `plugins.edi-loop.corpus` in
USER   `$HERMES_HOME/config.yaml` or `~/.hermes/config.yaml`. No homedir guessing: a wrong guess produces a silent
USER   stale build. The rules are in `tools/edi_corpus.py`: an empty `EDI_CORPUS` is unset; a set one that is not a
USER   corpus is an error naming the path and never falls through; the resolved rule prints `corpus source: …`; an
USER   unreadable Hermes config is reported with its path and reason.
USER - **No silent skips.** A check that needs the corpus and has none prints `SKIP[corpus] <id>: <reason>`;
USER   `PLUMBLINE_REQUIRE_CORPUS=1` (only `1`; `""`/`0` are off, anything else is a usage error) turns each into
USER   `FAIL[corpus] <id>`. `tests/run_all.py` prints one line per id and the `PLUMBLINE-TALLY` last.
USER   `kit/emit_packet.py`, `kit/emit_record.py` and `kit/plumbline_job.py` still need a corpus (without one,
USER   `emit_packet.py` refuses and `emit_record.py` exits 1 with its output unvalidated).
USER - **`tools/pin.json` is PLUMBLINE-relative** — it lives in this repository, and its `corpus` field names the
USER   corpus repository, not a local path. It does **not** exist in the corpus checkout, so a reader looking for the pin under `edi/` will not find it. The
USER   conformance runner resolves its `corpus:` surface entries through it.
USER 
USER **The one-directional invariant.** Plumbline is an instance, so it may constrain itself **more**, never
USER less. `tests/validate_schemas.py` runs the corpus's own fixture battery through both schemas and asserts
USER that **no fixture is accepted by Plumbline and rejected by the corpus** (the battery is the vendored copy;
USER with `EDI_CORPUS` set, `corpus.battery-equality` checks the copy is still the corpus's bytes). If that ever fires, Plumbline has
USER become its own law.
