USER # Plumbline — AGENTS.md
USER 
USER Session context for AI agents working in this repo. Read this before touching anything.
USER 
USER ## Project Overview
USER 
USER Plumbline is a **custody/v1 emitter plus an E-DI kit**: it writes and verifies custody/v1 records for a job's
USER work, and it carries software work from requirements elicitation through development, deployment and revision
USER so that every stage transition is gated by an evaluator independent of whoever produced the work. It was built
USER under, and runs well under, Hermes Agent; the Hermes-specific notes below apply only there.
USER 
USER It is **not** a pipeline framework, and it is **not** a second copy of the doctrine. Orchestration is reused
USER from the Kingsgate pattern (role-per-profile, a conductor script, `no_agent` crons). What Plumbline adds is
USER the **instance layer** — the labels, the standing frame, and the discoverability gate that E-DI deliberately
USER leaves to each deployment (Contracts §8) — plus the conformance audits that check real instances against the
USER doctrine.
USER 
USER The strategy documents (goals, architecture, decision log) are kept outside this repository.
USER 
USER ## The one rule that shapes everything
USER 
USER **A prompted model promises; a configured instance enforces.**
USER 
USER Contracts §3's must-ask list lives in a schema, not in an instruction. "While `ratified: false` the must-ask
USER fields must be absent" is a JSON Schema `if/then` — structurally impossible to violate. If you are about to add
USER a paragraph telling an agent to behave correctly, stop and ask whether it should be a schema constraint or a
USER hook instead.
USER 
USER ## The doctrine this obeys — seven weight-bearing files, authority by question
USER 
USER Plumbline's schemas are **derived from the public corpus `evaluatordriven/doctrine` at `e-di@f8bfb7934dbb`
USER (Contracts v1.27.0)**, its `contracts/` directory. Point `--corpus` or `$EDI_CORPUS` at
USER `<a checkout of it>/contracts`; `tools/pin.json` requires exactly that path. **When two files disagree,
USER `AUTHORITY.json` at the doctrine's top level says which wins**; read it before acting on a conflict. Record the
USER commit, not just a version: a version number cannot be checked, a commit can. Every derived schema names the pin.
USER **Re-pinning is a deliberate act, and the build refuses until you do it.** `tools/build_schemas.py` will not
USER run when the corpus has moved, because a silent stale build is the failure mode. Re-read the changed doctrine,
USER then `--repin`. A re-read under a moved rubric is a NEW job, never a correction of an old one — same runner
USER with a different rubric is a different instrument (C30).
USER 
USER **A changed derived hash is NOT a moved rubric.** The pin is embedded in every derived file's `description`, so
USER a pin-only bump changes all four hashes while the law is untouched. Measured 2026-09-30: the corpus moved only
USER to file an evaluation record and bump a skill pointer; four derived files changed hash and the sole diff in
USER each was `edi@318533b6bc00` -> `edi@2f8a54be9cae`, with the checklist's clause text and every `why`
USER annotation byte-identical. **Read the diff.** Treating a hash change as a rubric move would declare a new job
USER under C30 and wrongly invalidate an existing read.
USER 
USER Authority is **by question**, and the load order is published in exactly one place — `EDI-FOR-MACHINES.md`.
USER Do not invent another sequence.
USER 
USER | Question | Primary home |
USER |---|---|
USER | Purpose, values, behaviour, strategy, priority, **seat meanings** | `EDI-PLAYBOOK.md` (the clarity spine; changes least) |
USER | The corpus's own must / must not, failure conditions | `EDI-MANDATE.md` (the frame instrument, Contracts §1.7) |
USER | States, schemas, pass rule, NORMATIVE implementer law | `EDI-MACHINE-CONTRACTS.md` |
USER | **The form of one handoff** | `EDI-OPERATING-INSTRUMENT.md` (rank 2; loses to Contracts only on mechanics) |
USER | One worked job to copy | `EDI-EXAMPLE.md` |
USER | Agent load order and bans | `EDI-FOR-MACHINES.md` |
USER | Human on-ramp | `EDI-QUICKSTART.md` |
