USER   rule must not govern work that is not Plumbline's"). Arming and disarming are **live** — the plugin re-reads
USER   `config.yaml` on every call, so no restart is needed to change scope — but the hook receives only tool name,
USER   args, `turn_id` and `session_id`, so it has no job context to test. Until it is scoped properly, the
USER   mechanism is discipline: arm per job, disarm after.
USER - **A plugin's code binds at gateway start, and PM2 is the handle.** A plugin file swap needs a restart —
USER   there is no hot reload. `hermes gateway restart` does nothing while a detached gateway holds the profile
USER   (`hermes gateway status` reports "Gateway service is not loaded"); the live gateway is the PM2 app
USER   `hermes-gateway`, so `pm2 restart hermes-gateway` is the command that lands a plugin change. Related, and
USER   paid for once: the host calls a plugin **tool** as `handler(args_dict, **kwargs)` — the whole argument dict
USER   **positionally** — so a handler must normalise that shape *and* named keywords, or it dies on every call
USER   while a unit suite that calls it with keywords stays green.
USER - **A plugin install can be silently shadowed by its own backup.** A sibling directory inside
USER   `~/.hermes/plugins/` that carries a `plugin.yaml` **is a plugin**: discovery loads it, and the registry lets
USER   a same-name, same-toolset duplicate **overwrite silently** (`hermes-agent/tools/registry.py:89`) — so a
USER   `.bak-…` copy can win the registration and you will debug the old code while every disk check says the new
USER   one is installed. Measured 2026-10-01: the fixed gate kept failing with its pre-fix `TypeError` after a
USER   verified install and restart. Back up to `~/.hermes/backups/`. **The operating loop guards this now** —
USER   `plugins:plumbline-gate-single-install` and `plugins:no-duplicate-declarations` fail the run instead of
USER   leaving it to be remembered, and both were **proven red** against a planted duplicate before being trusted:
USER   `PLUMBLINE_PLUGINS_ROOT` is the seam that makes that proof possible (point it at a throwaway plugin home and
USER   watch them fail). A guard nobody has seen fail is a guard nobody knows works.
USER - **Headless runs cannot prompt.** An approval gate inside a cron run hangs until timeout. Human gates must be
USER   queues (delivered to a Telegram topic), never blocking prompts.
USER - **A bar that shells out to the emitter is green only on a COMMITTED tree.** The emitter refuses a dirty tree,
USER   because a record must name code that exists as a commit, so a Maker's uncommitted work makes its own bar
USER   unmeasurable and the failures read as capability failures. The loop commits the Maker's output before
USER   measuring, and no bar is judged red until the tree is clean. A bar may not write into the tree it measures —
USER   fixtures go to temp directories. Measured 2026-11-08 (job `evaluator-harness-log`): two failures looked like
USER   capability gaps and were the dirty tree; the fix was a commit, not code.
