USER ## The custody inputs: `inputs/custody.json`
USER 
USER Write this file **as events happen** (freeze, approvals, passes, controls), not afterwards. It lives under
USER `inputs/`, so the close walk skips it. Every path is relative to the job folder unless noted. The emitter computes
USER every digest from the files themselves, except approval digests, which you copy from what each approver approved.
USER 
USER A complete synthetic example is at `tests/fixtures/custody/job-rejected/inputs/custody.json`. Its shape:
USER 
USER ```jsonc
USER {
USER   "custody_inputs": "plumbline/custody-inputs/v1",
USER   "levelClaimed": "CL1",                       // CL2 later: add "spread", run >= 2, a second family
USER   "files_dir": "custody/files",                // default; the subject = every file here (or list "subject")
USER   "evidence_dir": "custody/evidence",          // default; evaluator replies + harness logs (names relative to it)
USER   "bar": {
USER     "file": "custody/bar.md",                  // the frozen bar text; bar.digest = sha256(this file)
USER     "frozenAt": "…Z",                          // must be before startedAt; log it live (custody_ledger.py freeze) or it is a hand time
USER     "author": "…", "publishedAt": null, "dissent": [], "groundTruth": [],
USER     "dimensions": [{"id": "D1", "plumbline_dimension": "<§5 dimension name>", "criterion": "…",
USER                     "scale": {"anchors": {"1": "…", "10": "…"}, "threshold": 9, "floor": 1},
USER                     "failureCondition": "…"}],
USER     "approvals": [{"role": "buyer", "by": "Steve Moitozo II", "kind": "person", "barDigest": "<hex the Buyer approved>", "at": "…Z", "adopted": null},
USER                   {"role": "specialist", "by": "<a person, not the orchestrator>", "kind": "person", "barDigest": "<hex>", "at": "…Z", "adopted": null}]   // log each with custody_ledger.py approve; a typed at is a hand time
USER   },
USER   "orchestrator": {"name": "…", "kind": "agent", "aliases": []},   // required: approvals are checked against it
USER   "routing": {"maker": {"via": "openrouter.ai", "provider": "…", "slug": "…", "host": "…", "declaredAt": "…Z"}},
USER                                                // written by custody_ledger.py pin, one per seat; must match the event log
USER   "maker": {"name": "…", "kind": "model", "version": "…", "method_file": "custody/method.md",
USER             "harness_log": "harness-maker-….log",  // default: the one harness-maker*.log in evidence_dir
USER             "model": {"family": "…", "identifier": "…", "readOnly": false}},   // endpoint: from the log
USER   "startedAt": "…Z", "producedAt": "…Z",       // leave unset: a typed value is a hand time (refused unless --allow-hand-times); the log supplies it
USER   "instrument": {"rubric_file": "…", "runner_file": "…"},   // optional when the §5 record pins both hashes
USER   "evaluators": [{"id": "eval-a", "name": "…", "kind": "model",
USER                   "harness_log": "harness-eval-….log",   // default: its CL1-15 external entry's evidence_file
USER                   "model": {"family": "…", "identifier": "…", "readOnly": true}}],
USER   "passes": {"<pass_id from run.json>": {"evaluator": "eval-a", "run": 1, "batch": "b1",
USER              "call": "eval_artifact",          // the harness call this pass is; time and endpoint come from it
USER              "evidence": {"D1": "<file in evidence_dir>"}}},
USER   "checks": [],                                // readings jobs (no §5 record): custody checks directly,
USER                                                // with "evidence_file" in place of "evidence"
USER   "controls": [{"id": "K1", "batch": "b1", "evaluator": "eval-a", "call": "eval_k1", "case_file": "…", "expected": "conforms", "outcome": "…",
USER                 "evidence_file": "…"},
USER                {"id": "K2", "batch": "b1", "evaluator": "eval-a", "case_file": "…", "expected": "does_not_conform", "outcome": "…"}],
USER   "violations": {"V1": {"dimension": "D2",     // §5 violations name no dimension: say which bar dimension
USER                         "remedy": {"text": "<verbatim from the evidence>", "evidence_file": "…"}}},  // optional
USER   "external": [{"requirement": "CL1-15", "subject": "eval-a", "evidence_file": "<harness log>", "readBy": "…"}],
USER   "retention": {"location": "…", "public": false},
USER   "disposition": {"by": "Steve Moitozo II", "at": "…Z", "acknowledgesUnobserved": []},   // log it live (custody_ledger.py dispose); a typed at is a hand time
USER   "verdict": "accept"                          // readings jobs only; scored jobs take it from the §5 record
USER }
USER ```
USER 
USER The emitter also accepts an optional top-level `"x-plumbline"` object here. It is copied **deep-equal** into the
USER record's `predicate["x-plumbline"].inputs`; it never shadows the emitter's own keys (`verdict`, `gaps`, `times`,
USER `hand_times`, `allowances`, `generations`, `evaluations`, `relays`, `amendments`, `spend`, `schema`), which the
USER verifier re-derives from the record, ledger and logs.
USER 
USER Rules the emitter enforces:
USER 
USER - A scored check's outcome is `score >= threshold` (CL1-17).
USER - A pass that is void, or a dimension with no score, becomes `unobserved` with its channel and reason.
USER - A remedy is kept only if it appears verbatim in the named evidence file.
USER - If no disposition time is recorded, `at` is the emission time, and the record says so (`x-plumbline.disposition_at_source`).
USER - Every time, endpoint, approver and revision rule in "Refusals" above, including hand times.
USER 
USER ## Logging events live: `kit/custody_ledger.py`
USER 
USER Freeze, approval, provider pins, disposition and the batch's own events (generations, evaluations, amendments,
USER relays, spend) have no harness log. Stamp each **at the moment it happens**; the tool writes it into
USER `inputs/custody.json` and appends it to `inputs/custody-events.jsonl`, and the emitter then refuses a ledger value
USER that differs from the logged one, and a bar that changed after its logged freeze.
USER 
USER Every line any command appends carries `writer: "kit/custody_ledger.py@<sha12>"` (the sha256 of the file that ran)
USER and `at` (the tool's clock), so a reader can tell a tool stamp from a hand-typed one. Every command appends
USER **exactly one** line when it succeeds; the earlier lines are never rewritten; a refused call appends nothing and
USER exits non-zero (2 = usage, 3 = refused), printing a line starting `REFUSED:` on stderr and leaving the ledger and
USER `custody.json` byte-identical.
USER 
USER Base commands:
USER 
USER ```sh
USER python3 kit/custody_ledger.py freeze  --job-dir J --orchestrator "VizH (Chief of Staff)" --orchestrator-alias VizH
USER python3 kit/custody_ledger.py approve --job-dir J --role buyer      --by "Steve Moitozo II"
USER python3 kit/custody_ledger.py approve --job-dir J --role specialist --by "<the Specialist, a person>"
USER python3 kit/custody_ledger.py pin     --job-dir J --seat maker  --provider … --slug … --host …   # before the Maker starts
USER python3 kit/custody_ledger.py pin     --job-dir J --seat eval-a --provider … --slug … --host …
USER python3 kit/custody_ledger.py dispose --job-dir J --by "Steve Moitozo II" [--state given|declined] \
USER     [--decided-at T --decided-source S] [--words W]
USER python3 kit/custody_ledger.py show    --job-dir J
USER ```
USER 
USER `approve` records the bar file's sha256 at that moment as the digest approved, and refuses the orchestrator, a
USER model, or an unmarked agent. Both approvals must be stamped **before the maker's first call**.
USER 
USER New commands (each appends one line, which the emitter reads into `x-plumbline`):
USER 
USER | command | appends | bounds |
USER |---|---|---|
USER | `generation open --seat S --prompt FILE` | `event:"generation", action:"open", seat, n, prompt_file, prompt_sha256` | `n` starts at 1 and goes up by 1; a second open while one is open is refused; the prompt's sha256 is computed by the tool |
USER | `generation close --seat S --n N [--outcome TEXT] [--fix-kind TEXT]` | `event:"generation", action:"close", seat, n, outcome, fix_kind` | a close without a matching open (same n, same seat) is refused |
USER | `evaluation --batch B --evaluator ID --verdict FILE [--summary FILE] [--iteration N]` | `event:"evaluation", batch, evaluator, verdict_file, verdict_sha256`; with `--summary`, also `summary_file, summary_sha256, started_at, finished_at, summary_writer` | the tool computes the verdict's sha256 and refuses a missing file; with `--summary` it takes `started_at`/`finished_at`/`writer` from the instrument's own stamp and refuses a summary with no stamp |
USER | `amendment --file F --quote Q --channel C --said-at T` | `event:"amendment", file, file_sha256, quote, channel, said_at` | the tool computes the file's sha256; refuses F inside `custody/`; T before the freeze or after the tool's clock is refused; the quote is stored verbatim |
USER | `relay --quote Q --from BOT --source-chat C --source-at T` | `event:"relay", kind:"relay", quote, from, source_chat, source_at` | the quote is stored verbatim and marked relay; T is bounded as in `amendment`; the emitter never counts a relay as an approval |
USER | `spend --spend-ledger L --key-reading R` | `event:"spend", spend_ledger, spend_ledger_sha256, key_reading, key_reading_sha256, ledger_sum_usd, key_delta_usd, total_usd, read_at, last_settle_at` | refuses a reading R with no `read_at`/`writer` stamp, or one read less than 3 min after the last `settle` line; both files' sha256 go in the line |
USER | `dispose --by NAME --state given\|declined --decided-at T --decided-source S [--words W]` | `event:"dispose", by, state, decided_at, decided_source, words`; `custody.json` `disposition.at = decided_at` | T earlier than the last logged check or later than the tool's clock is refused; T equal to the last check is accepted |
USER 
USER The emitter reads each line into `x-plumbline`: `generations[]`, `evaluations[]`, `relays[]`, `amendments[]` and
USER `spend`, each with a `source` of `inputs/custody-events.jsonl:<line>`.
USER 
USER ## The product harness: `tools/edi/`
USER 
USER The kit's own product harness lives under `tools/edi/`. It is standard library only, and every instrument that
USER writes a summary stamps `started_at`, `finished_at` (UTC Z, its own clock) and `writer: "tools/edi/<file>@<sha12>"`.
USER 
USER - `tools/edi/or_call.py` — one logged OpenRouter chat call for a seat, in job 1's harness-log shape so
USER   `kit/custody_history.read_log` can read it. A job spend file (`--job-spend`) gives the job id, bound, reserve,
USER   spend ledger and price snapshot, with no job hard-coded; the call is refused when
USER   `total + worst case + reserve >= bound`. `--max-cost X` refuses before sending when the worst case is greater
USER   than X, and exits 9 after settle when `total_cost` is greater than X. `--init-spend` writes the key-usage
USER   baseline, and `--spend-status --out R` writes the stamped key/ledger reading that `custody_ledger.py spend`
USER   records. `--mock-transport DIR` keeps everything off the network for tests. Exit codes: 0 served, 2
USER   usage/key/job-spend error, 3 HTTP/transport, 4 pin refusal, 5 no generation reply, 6 bound refusal, 8
USER   `--max-cost` refusal before sending, 9 over the cap after settle.
USER - `tools/edi/judge_batch.py` — a model-judge batch: one `or_call.py` per item, the order for batch N rotated left
USER   by `(N-1) mod len`, with `summary.json` carrying the batch and the `order`.
USER - `tools/edi/fixture_run.py` — copies a fixture tree into a working directory, makes every copied file and
USER   directory owner-writable whatever the source modes, and writes a summary of paths, sha256 and mode.
USER - `tools/edi/extract_files.py` — the whole-file reply extractor, with `--check` (verify only) and without it
USER   (write).
USER 
USER ## Running the three jobs
USER 
USER For each job:
USER 
USER 1. **Open** the job: `python3 kit/plumbline_job.py open --brief brief.json --job-dir dot-plumbline/jobs/<id> --corpus <doctrine@f8bfb793>/contracts`.
USER 2. **Freeze** the bar: write `custody/bar.md`, then `custody_ledger.py freeze` at that moment. The Buyer and the Specialist (each a person; never the orchestrator) each approve it, stamped with `custody_ledger.py approve` as they do, before any maker call.
USER 3. **Pin** (OpenRouter): the Orchestrator chooses one ZDR provider per model from `GET https://openrouter.ai/api/v1/endpoints/zdr` and declares each seat's pin with `custody_ledger.py pin` **before the Maker starts**. Use the exact model slugs (see "Provider pin and routing request"). Every request body carries `"provider": {"order": ["<pinned slug>"], "allow_fallbacks": false, "zdr": true}`. Log every call in full: the whole request body, the whole response, the `Date` header, and the `/api/v1/generation` reply under `generation`.
USER 4. **Make**: run the maker model and put the artifact in `custody/files/`. Its run file names the artifact the §5 record hashes. Save the maker's harness log (with its `Date` header and response) as `custody/evidence/harness-maker-<model>.log`. Record the maker block; do not type `startedAt`/`producedAt` (the log decides).
USER 5. **Evaluate**: per batch, run the evaluator on the artifact and on a clean and a planted control case.
USER    - Save each reply into `custody/evidence/`.
USER    - Save the harness log, one named call per pass and control (`eval_artifact`, `eval_k1`, `eval_k2`), into `custody/evidence/`.
USER    - Fill `passes` and `controls`, each with its `call`; do not type their times.
USER    - Write the iteration's `run.json` (kit/emit_record.py's shape) with the scores and verdict.
USER    - Log the batch's own events with the ledger: `custody_ledger.py generation open/close` around each Maker
USER      generation, `custody_ledger.py evaluation` for each verdict file (with `--summary` when an instrument wrote
USER      one), and `custody_ledger.py amendment`/`relay` for anything a person said. `custody_ledger.py spend` after
USER      the last settle.
USER 6. **Close** at the terminal state. This also writes the custody record and runs the verifier:
USER 
USER    ```sh
USER    python3 kit/plumbline_job.py close --job-dir dot-plumbline/jobs/<id> --run run-1.json \
USER      --corpus <doctrine@f8bfb793>/contracts --terminal-state <accepted|needs_owner|rejected> --outcome <same> \
USER      [--disposition given|declined] [--allow-hand-times] [--custody <custody checkout>]
USER    ```
USER 
USER    | Terminal state | Close flags |
USER    |---|---|
USER    | `accepted` | verdict `accept`, every dimension at or above threshold, `--terminal-state accepted --outcome accepted`. |
USER    | `rejected` | verdict `reject`, at least one `does_not_conform` dimension with a violation and its locator, `--terminal-state rejected --outcome rejected`. |
USER    | `needs_owner` | verdict `needs_owner` (e.g. cap reached or a capability gap), `--terminal-state needs_owner --outcome needs_owner --escalation-kind cap_reached`. |
USER 
USER    Across the three records, at least one violation must exist (xlocator).
USER 
USER    `close` runs the emitter, then `kit/plumbline_verify.py` on the new record. If the verifier fails, close exits
USER    with the verifier's code (3 or 4), prints `NOT CLOSED: Plumbline verify failed (<kind>)`, writes a run-log row
USER    `close_refused` with the kind, and writes no `closed` row; re-running close after a fix retries. `--custody`
USER    defaults to `$PLUMBLINE_CUSTODY`. `--allow-hand-times` is passed to the emitter and affects only hand-time
USER    refusals.
USER 
USER    Emit from a **clean checkout** of the stated revision, with `PLUMBLINE_EXPECT_REVISION=<tree sha>` set.
USER 
USER 7. **Re-emit** without a corpus, any time. `custody` runs the emitter, then the verifier, and returns its code:
USER 
USER    ```sh
USER    python3 kit/plumbline_job.py custody --job-dir dot-plumbline/jobs/<id> [--out-dir DIR] \
USER      [--expect-revision <tree>] [--allow-hand-times] [--custody <checkout>]
USER    ```
USER 
USER    Then check the record at both layers, if the custody checkout is present:
USER 
USER    ```sh
USER    python3 kit/plumbline_verify.py --record dot-plumbline/jobs/<id>/custody/record.custody.json \
USER      --files dot-plumbline/jobs/<id>/custody/files --evidence dot-plumbline/jobs/<id>/custody/evidence \
USER      --ledger dot-plumbline/jobs/<id>/inputs/custody-events.jsonl --custody <custody checkout>
USER    ```
USER 
USER    The standard layer alone (check.py) is:
USER 
USER    ```sh
USER    python3 custody/checker/check.py dot-plumbline/jobs/<id>/custody/record.custody.json \
USER      --files dot-plumbline/jobs/<id>/custody/files --evidence dot-plumbline/jobs/<id>/custody/evidence \
USER      --bar dot-plumbline/jobs/<id>/custody/bar.md
USER    ```
USER 
USER ## Where outputs land
USER 
USER | Output | Path |
USER |---|---|
USER | Record | `dot-plumbline/jobs/<id>/custody/record.custody.json`, or `<out-dir>/<job_id>.custody.json` with `--out-dir`. |
USER | Bar, subject, evidence and controls | `dot-plumbline/jobs/<id>/custody/{bar.md, files/, evidence/, controls/}`. These are the paths for `records[]`: `files` = `…/custody/files`, `evidence` = `…/custody/evidence`, `bar` = `…/custody/bar.md`. |
USER | Run-log row | Unchanged (`event: closed`); `close_refused` with the kind when the verifier refuses. |
USER 
USER ## Packing a stranger bundle
USER 
USER ```sh
USER python3 tools/pack_stranger_bundle.py --job-dir dot-plumbline/jobs/<id> --out <dir>/<id>.zip
USER ```
USER 
USER The zip contains:
USER 
USER | Entry | Contents |
USER |---|---|
USER | `record.custody.json` | The record. |
USER | `bar/<bar file>` | The frozen bar. |
USER | `files/` | The subject files. |
USER | `evidence/` | Every evidence file and harness log. |
USER | `context/` | The method, instrument and control files, the §5 record and the packet. |
USER | `MANIFEST.sha256` | sha256 manifest, `sha256sum -c` compatible. |
USER | `README.txt` | The one check command. |
USER 
USER Packing is deterministic. The command prints the zip's sha256 and the in-zip paths:
USER `record.custody.json`, `files`, `evidence`, `bar/<name>`.
USER 
USER ## Drift check
USER 
USER ```sh
USER CUSTODY_DIR=<custody checkout at the pin> python3 tools/build_schemas.py --custody --check --require-upstream
USER ```
USER 
USER - Exits 1 if the checkout moved (commit, or the sha256 of schema/check.py/SPEC.md), if the vendored `schemas/custody/custody-v1.schema.json` is not the pinned bytes, or if `schemas/custody-record.schema.json` is not exactly what the derivation gives (a hand edit).
USER - Exits 2 with no checkout under `--require-upstream`.
USER - Re-derive after a deliberate move with `--custody --repin`.
