USER # Plumbline — AGENTS.md
USER 
USER Session context for AI agents working in this repo. Read this before touching anything.
USER 
USER ## Project Overview
USER 
USER Plumbline is a **custody/v1 emitter plus an E-DI kit**: it writes and verifies custody/v1 records for a job's
USER work, and it carries software work from requirements elicitation through development, deployment and revision
USER so that every stage transition is gated by an evaluator independent of whoever produced the work. It was built
USER under, and runs well under, Hermes Agent; the Hermes-specific notes below apply only there.
USER 
USER It is **not** a pipeline framework, and it is **not** a second copy of the doctrine. Orchestration is reused
USER from the Kingsgate pattern (role-per-profile, a conductor script, `no_agent` crons). What Plumbline adds is
USER the **instance layer** — the labels, the standing frame, and the discoverability gate that E-DI deliberately
USER leaves to each deployment (Contracts §8) — plus the conformance audits that check real instances against the
USER doctrine.
USER 
USER The strategy documents (goals, architecture, decision log) are kept outside this repository.
USER 
USER ## The one rule that shapes everything
USER 
USER **A prompted model promises; a configured instance enforces.**
USER 
USER Contracts §3's must-ask list lives in a schema, not in an instruction. "While `ratified: false` the must-ask
USER fields must be absent" is a JSON Schema `if/then` — structurally impossible to violate. If you are about to add
USER a paragraph telling an agent to behave correctly, stop and ask whether it should be a schema constraint or a
USER hook instead.
USER 
USER ## The doctrine this obeys — seven weight-bearing files, authority by question
USER 
USER Plumbline's schemas are **derived from the public corpus `evaluatordriven/doctrine` at `e-di@f8bfb7934dbb`
USER (Contracts v1.27.0)**, its `contracts/` directory. Point `--corpus` or `$EDI_CORPUS` at
USER `<a checkout of it>/contracts`; `tools/pin.json` requires exactly that path. **When two files disagree,
USER `AUTHORITY.json` at the doctrine's top level says which wins**; read it before acting on a conflict. Record the
USER commit, not just a version: a version number cannot be checked, a commit can. Every derived schema names the pin.
USER **Re-pinning is a deliberate act, and the build refuses until you do it.** `tools/build_schemas.py` will not
USER run when the corpus has moved, because a silent stale build is the failure mode. Re-read the changed doctrine,
USER then `--repin`. A re-read under a moved rubric is a NEW job, never a correction of an old one — same runner
USER with a different rubric is a different instrument (C30).
USER 
USER **A changed derived hash is NOT a moved rubric.** The pin is embedded in every derived file's `description`, so
USER a pin-only bump changes all four hashes while the law is untouched. Measured 2026-09-30: the corpus moved only
USER to file an evaluation record and bump a skill pointer; four derived files changed hash and the sole diff in
USER each was `edi@318533b6bc00` -> `edi@2f8a54be9cae`, with the checklist's clause text and every `why`
USER annotation byte-identical. **Read the diff.** Treating a hash change as a rubric move would declare a new job
USER under C30 and wrongly invalidate an existing read.
USER 
USER Authority is **by question**, and the load order is published in exactly one place — `EDI-FOR-MACHINES.md`.
USER Do not invent another sequence.
USER 
USER | Question | Primary home |
USER |---|---|
USER | Purpose, values, behaviour, strategy, priority, **seat meanings** | `EDI-PLAYBOOK.md` (the clarity spine; changes least) |
USER | The corpus's own must / must not, failure conditions | `EDI-MANDATE.md` (the frame instrument, Contracts §1.7) |
USER | States, schemas, pass rule, NORMATIVE implementer law | `EDI-MACHINE-CONTRACTS.md` |
USER | **The form of one handoff** | `EDI-OPERATING-INSTRUMENT.md` (rank 2; loses to Contracts only on mechanics) |
USER | One worked job to copy | `EDI-EXAMPLE.md` |
USER | Agent load order and bans | `EDI-FOR-MACHINES.md` |
USER | Human on-ramp | `EDI-QUICKSTART.md` |
USER 
USER **Not authority, ever:** `evidence/**`, `proposals/**`, `evaluation/**` (unless the job *is* to audit them),
USER `EDI-PRIMER.md`, `EDI-ARCHITECTURE.md`, `EDI-CHANGELOG.md`, Applied/Research narratives. The Primer explains
USER *why*; it is not law. Implementing from an Applied narrative is the documented failure mode — it promotes
USER examples to defaults.
USER 
USER A conflict between the Playbook and the Contracts on **seats or purpose** escalates to the Owner. On
USER **states or schema**, obey the Contracts and report the conflict. Never quiet-edit the Playbook down to match
USER a projection.
USER 
USER ## How the schemas are made — derived, never hand-edited
USER 
USER Plumbline no longer authors its own field names. It shipped a second job-packet schema that disagreed with
USER the corpus's own (measured: the corpus's fixtures give 18 errors against it). Two live schemas for one law
USER means neither is authority, and a hand-copied schema drifts silently.
USER 
USER ```
USER tools/build_schemas.py   corpus @ pin  +  schemas/instance-extensions.json
USER                          -> schemas/job-packet.schema.json       (corpus §3/§6 + instance layer)
USER                          -> schemas/evaluator-record.schema.json (corpus §5, shape-for-shape)
USER                          -> schemas/audit-record.schema.json     (corpus §5 + required audit extensions)
USER                          -> schemas/compliance-checklist.json    (Contracts §9, `why` re-attached by id)
USER                          -> tools/pin.json                       (commit + every source file's sha256)
USER ```
USER 
USER - **`schemas/instance-extensions.json` is the only place instance law is written.** Everything else in
USER   `schemas/` is derived and must never be edited by hand — a hand edit is erased by the next build and is
USER   invisible in review.
USER - **The build refuses to run when the corpus has moved.** Re-read the changed doctrine, then `--repin`.
USER   `--check` verifies the pin without writing; run `tests/run_all.py` before every commit (CI runs it too,
USER   with no corpus).
USER - **The corpus is vendored at the pin.** `schemas/vendor/e-di@f8bfb793/` holds the corpus's two schemas and
USER   its fixture battery, byte for byte, with `VENDOR.json` (provenance, every sha256). The battery runs on it, so
USER   the suites need no corpus; `tools/build_schemas.py --check` fails when the copy, `VENDOR.json` and
USER   `tools/pin.json` disagree. Never edit the copy; re-vendor it from the corpus when the pin moves.
USER - **Corpus resolution follows the edi-loop plugin's own rule** — `--corpus`, else `EDI_CORPUS`, else (under
USER   Hermes, in `tools/build_schemas.py` and `kit/plumbline.py`) `plugins.edi-loop.corpus` in
USER   `$HERMES_HOME/config.yaml` or `~/.hermes/config.yaml`. No homedir guessing: a wrong guess produces a silent
USER   stale build. The rules are in `tools/edi_corpus.py`: an empty `EDI_CORPUS` is unset; a set one that is not a
USER   corpus is an error naming the path and never falls through; the resolved rule prints `corpus source: …`; an
USER   unreadable Hermes config is reported with its path and reason.
USER - **No silent skips.** A check that needs the corpus and has none prints `SKIP[corpus] <id>: <reason>`;
USER   `PLUMBLINE_REQUIRE_CORPUS=1` (only `1`; `""`/`0` are off, anything else is a usage error) turns each into
USER   `FAIL[corpus] <id>`. `tests/run_all.py` prints one line per id and the `PLUMBLINE-TALLY` last.
USER   `kit/emit_packet.py`, `kit/emit_record.py` and `kit/plumbline_job.py` still need a corpus (without one,
USER   `emit_packet.py` refuses and `emit_record.py` exits 1 with its output unvalidated).
USER - **`tools/pin.json` is PLUMBLINE-relative** — it lives in this repository, and its `corpus` field names the
USER   corpus repository, not a local path. It does **not** exist in the corpus checkout, so a reader looking for the pin under `edi/` will not find it. The
USER   conformance runner resolves its `corpus:` surface entries through it.
USER 
USER **The one-directional invariant.** Plumbline is an instance, so it may constrain itself **more**, never
USER less. `tests/validate_schemas.py` runs the corpus's own fixture battery through both schemas and asserts
USER that **no fixture is accepted by Plumbline and rejected by the corpus** (the battery is the vendored copy;
USER with `EDI_CORPUS` set, `corpus.battery-equality` checks the copy is still the corpus's bytes). If that ever fires, Plumbline has
USER become its own law.
USER 
USER ## Key invariants from the doctrine (do not weaken)
USER 
USER - **Roles separate, and the seats are seven:** Owner, Architect, Buyer, Specialist, Maker, Evaluator,
USER   Orchestrator (Playbook §6, Contracts §4). One party may hold several — **recorded as such**. The Owner may
USER   never hold the Evaluator seat on the same job. The Maker never grades its own work.
USER - **The Evaluator recommends; the Orchestrator applies.** Only the Orchestrator applies the pass rule, the
USER   cap, and escalation. An Evaluator that decides whether to iterate has collapsed two roles. Applying
USER   escalation means handing `needs_owner` to the Architect, which writes the options and the recommendation;
USER   the Orchestrator delivers them to the Owner and writes none of it. **Plumbline rule** (Owner ruling
USER   2026-11-04, `schemas/instance-extensions.json` → `escalation_pen`): no doctrine tier names this pen, and
USER   Contracts §4's "recommends … and the Architect decides" is about a design gap, not `needs_owner`.
USER - **Pass rule = `mean >= T_accept` AND `min >= T_dim`.** An average alone lets a fatal defect be averaged
USER   away.
USER - **A verdict needs ≥2 scored runs.** One run is a sample, not a measurement, and a reading resting on one
USER   run is labelled as such (Instrument rule 10).
USER - **Terminal states are exactly `accepted`, `needs_owner`, `rejected`** — and a record carries the **outcome**
USER   it exited under: `accepted` · `census` · `needs_owner` · `rejected` · `no_observation` · `no_artifact`.
USER   An outcome is not a state. Cap-reached above the floor is `needs_owner`, **not** `rejected` — routing it to
USER   `rejected` discards work a human was meant to see.
USER - **`mode: scored` requires a Specialist named at the outset.** With none, the job declares `mode: readings`,
USER   supplies **no thresholds**, returns *conforms / does not conform / unobserved* and **never numbers**, and
USER   **may not produce `rejected`** — there is no floor to be below. Naming a Specialist after the readings are
USER   in does not license scoring.
USER - **A dimension with no observation ⇒ forced `needs_owner`,** never `rejected`, never a synthesised
USER   below-floor score. `observation` is required by the schema, so this is structural.
USER - **A dimension that saw ground truth is invalid.** `saw_gt` fails that dimension (§7.2).
USER - **Entry is decided by the artifact, not by intent.** Work that already exists is Entry B — a labelled
USER   baseline (C32) that is legal only when `reads_before_freeze` names the reads that could reveal a defect and
USER   states they came after the rubric was frozen. A fix is a **new job**.
USER - **Violations are classified into rule 4's four locators** — `artifact_missed_standard`, `standard_moved`,
USER   `dimension_unauthorized`, `frame_at_fault` — never "bad Maker". **There is no severity scale**: `P0/P1/P2`
USER   are undefined and must not be copied or invented. The shortfall is required; the **remedy is optional**,
USER   and a fabricated remedy is itself a violation.
USER - **The instrument is retained with the artifact**, and it is **both** the rubric and the code: the record
USER   names its version and the sha256 of rubric **and** runner (C30). A re-run holding the same rubric and a
USER   different runner is a different instrument.
USER - **A revision carries a control in the same batch.** Comparing against a stored score reports the
USER   instrument's drift as change in the artifact — measured: a document scored 8.64 returned 8.33/8.14/8.00/
USER   7.86/8.39 on byte-identical input.
USER - **Carry the best parts forward.** The standing artifact is the frontier; the **best** artifact is the
USER   Maker's input, with every known defect and its measurement. A trade is the Owner's to declare.
USER - **Verdict and disposition are separate acts.** The Owner may ship a reject or bin an accept; the recorded
USER   verdict stands as the loop produced it.
USER - **External writes are verified by read-back,** with a normalization declared before comparing and a value
USER   fresh on every run (§7.3).
