USER ## Enforcement available to this instance
USER 
USER The doctrine's public checkers in `evaluatordriven/doctrine` `contracts/tools/` (pin `f8bfb79`, v0.2.1) are
USER the four deterministic checks — `validate_job_packet.py`, `check_terminal_state.py`,
USER `check_role_separation.py`, `check_best_artifact_handoff.py`. **Use them; do not re-implement them here.**
USER (The older `edi-loop` Hermes plugin wrapped them; its corpus copy no longer exists and nothing depends on it.)
USER The skill is `evaluator-driven-iteration`.
USER 
USER **One gate.** Plumbline validates against the corpus's `tools/validate_job_packet.py`. A private jsonschema
USER pass is **not** a census of the corpus. `EDI-FOR-MACHINES.md:79` is the corpus-side rule, and it is **not
USER restated here** — *"`tools/validate_job_packet.py` is the full structural gate and covers the remaining object
USER shapes. Trust the checker, not the schema alone."* Cite it by location rather than copying it: a restatement
USER is a second place to go stale, which is the argument proposal 0001 already made about this file.
USER 
USER **Surface completeness.** The observed surface of any conformance read must **contain the enforcement layer**
USER — the gate, not only the schemas. A finding whose subject is a schema-versus-clause divergence is **not a
USER corpus finding until the gate has been run on it.** Measured 2026-09-30: with `escalation: ""` the gate exits
USER 1 while a bare jsonschema pass reports **zero** errors over the same packet — so a read that observes only
USER schemas will report the schema's looseness as a defect in the doctrine. It did exactly that on 2026-09-29,
USER and the finding had to be withdrawn.
USER 
USER **Why this is not local hygiene.** A census scored against the schema while the corpus gate enforces is
USER running a **second ruler**. The rule set on 2026-09-29 applies unchanged — *the check that can override the
USER Evaluator is the Evaluator* — and a census that quietly outranks the corpus gate has become the corpus's real
USER evaluator.
USER 
USER **Three passes measure consistency, never correctness.** The ~25% clause spread below says one pass is not a
USER verdict; it does **not** say that agreement makes a reading true. The withdrawn C33 finding was stable across
USER **all three** passes — the instrument agreed with itself and was wrong together, because the surface omitted
USER the gate. **Repetition cannot detect a blind spot.** Check the surface before trusting the agreement.
USER 
USER **Run the gate, do not argue about it.** Dispositions for C5, C16 and C32 were settled on 2026-09-30 by
USER constructing each state the finding names and running the gate on it — never by reasoning that no gate could
USER bear on them. Inspection had already produced C20, the failure-test premise and C33, and it was wrong about
USER C5 and C16 too (it would have claimed the gate enforces neither; it in fact enforces cap/budget presence and
USER the 0028 A seat rule). Both probes are retained under the job's `instrument/`.
USER 
USER ## Profile roster
USER 
USER **The Evaluator seat is built and has run a job.** `plumbline-eval` — model `z-ai/glm-5.3-flash` via provider
USER `openrouter`, a **different model family** from the Maker (DeepSeek), per rule 2. Smoke-tested before the job.
USER 
USER - **THE `base_url` TRAP, on every new seat.** A new profile inherits `model.base_url` from `default`. With
USER   `model.provider: openrouter` and an inherited `base_url: https://api.deepseek.com/v1`, GLM requests would
USER   have been routed to **DeepSeek's own server** — role separation nominally satisfied and actually violated,
USER   because the "independent" evaluator would have run on the maker's infrastructure. Clear `model.base_url`
USER   when pinning a seat, and verify the seat answers before building anything on it.
USER - **The seat is a heavy reasoner.** ~6.5k reasoning tokens per clause plus the surface, so 33 clauses in one
USER   call needs ~200k and never completes (`finish_reason: length`, empty content). Chunk the clauses; bound
USER   thinking with `reasoning: {max_tokens: N}`.
USER 
USER The remaining seats are unbuilt. The seat set is Playbook §6's seven; these are the profiles that would carry
USER them (one profile per role, model pinned per profile — the §4 role-independence mechanism).
USER 
USER | Profile | Seat | Tools | Notes |
USER |---|---|---|---|
USER | `<program>-conductor` | Orchestrator | none | Routes, enforces bounds, applies the pass rule, runs the discoverability gate; hands `needs_owner` to the Architect and delivers its options. Decides nothing. |
USER | `<program>-maker` | Maker | file, terminal (write) | Works in a git worktree |
USER | `<program>-eval` | Evaluator | file, terminal (read/run) | **Different model family** from the Maker. No write tools. |
USER | `<program>-deploy` | Deployment | terminal, web | Read-back verification scripts |
USER | `<program>-audit` | Auditor | file, terminal | Job-record audit + discoverable-escalation rate |
USER 
USER **Constraint:** `delegate_task` children all inherit one model from `delegation.*` config — there is no
USER per-child model. Role separation therefore requires separate profiles/processes, not subagents. This also
USER preserves prompt caching.
