USER # Custody emission runbook — custody/v1 records from Plumbline jobs
USER 
USER For the Orchestrator. This page covers how to run small real jobs that end `accepted`, `needs_owner` and
USER `rejected`, collect one custody/v1 record from each, and pack each record for a stranger. Plumbline makes no
USER model call on its own: every model call is one you make, through your own harness, or through the kit's product
USER harness `tools/edi/or_call.py` (see "The product harness: `tools/edi/`").
USER 
USER A record is judged at **two layers**. The **standard layer** is the custody corpus's own `checker/check.py` at
USER CL1; per SPEC.md line 141 it ignores every key that begins with `x-`. The **Plumbline layer** is
USER `kit/plumbline_verify.py`: it runs `check.py` first, requires OK, and then enforces the `x-plumbline` block the
USER emitter writes. `x-plumbline` is enforced by Plumbline's verifier only; the standard check.py ignores it. See
USER "The two layers" below.
USER 
USER ## What a close now writes
USER 
USER A job that closes at a terminal state writes everything it wrote before, unchanged: the §5 records, `passes/`,
USER the packet facts, the run-log row and `inputs/`. It then writes one more file:
USER 
USER     dot-plumbline/jobs/<job-id>/custody/record.custody.json
USER 
USER That file is an in-toto Statement with predicateType `https://evaluatordriven.com/custody/v1`, following custody
USER SPEC.md v0.9.6 at `evaluatordriven/custody@3e3ca58de563`. The predicate holds only section-4 fields. Plumbline's
USER own facts sit under `predicate["x-plumbline"]` and the per-check, per-control and per-violation `x-plumbline`
USER keys:
USER 
USER - `schema` (`"plumbline/x-plumbline/v1"`), `verdict` and `terminal_state`.
USER - `outcome`.
USER - The final §5 record and the packet, each by sha256.
USER - Each violation's rule-4 `locator`.
USER - `gaps`, `times`, `time_tolerance_s`.
USER - `hand_times[]` — every hand time the record carries, each `{field, file, line, source}` — and `allowances`
USER   (`hand_times: {switch, writer, at}` only when `--allow-hand-times` was given and the set is not empty).
USER - `generations[]`, `evaluations[]`, `relays[]`, `amendments[]` and `spend` — the ledger's own events, each with
USER   its `inputs/custody-events.jsonl:<line>` source (see "Logging events live").
USER - `inputs` — a deep copy of the `x-plumbline` object in `inputs/custody.json`, when there is one. Input data
USER   appears only under `x-plumbline.inputs`; it never shadows the emitter's own keys.
USER 
USER `disposition.state` is copied from the packet's `disposition_state`. It never comes from the verdict.
USER 
USER | Exit code | Meaning |
USER |---|---|
USER | 0 | Complete. |
USER | 1 | Written, with a hard gap named in `x-plumbline.gaps`: a required fact had no source (left null/absent; fix the inputs and re-emit), or an OpenRouter routing request that did not ask for the pin, no fallback and ZDR (`routing.<seat>.request`; the run did it, so re-emitting cannot fix it). |
USER | 2 | Refused. There is no packet, or the job is not at a terminal state. Nothing written. |
USER | 3 | **Refused: the record would misstate its own history** (see "Refusals" below). This includes every hand time when `--allow-hand-times` is not given (`hand-time`). Every such fact is printed as `[kind] …`; nothing is written and an existing record is left as it was. `close` still closes the job and returns 3, with "CLOSED, BUT THE CUSTODY RECORD IS NOT WRITTEN". |
USER 
USER `plumbline_job.py close` runs the emitter, then `kit/plumbline_verify.py` on the new record. If the verifier
USER fails, close exits with the verifier's code (3 or 4), prints `NOT CLOSED: Plumbline verify failed (<kind>)`,
USER writes a run-log row `close_refused` with the kind, and writes **no** `closed` row; re-running close after a fix
USER retries. `plumbline_job.py custody` (re-emit) runs the verifier too and returns its code. Both accept
USER `--allow-hand-times` (passed to the emitter) and `--custody <checkout>` (default: env `PLUMBLINE_CUSTODY`).
USER 
USER ## The two layers
USER 
USER The **standard layer** is the custody corpus's `checker/check.py`, run at the claimed level (CL1). It checks the
USER custody/v1 predicate. Per SPEC.md line 141 it **ignores every key that begins with `x-`**, so it never reads
USER `x-plumbline` — no matter what the extension says. A record that passes `check.py` has passed the standard layer
USER and nothing more.
USER 
USER The **Plumbline layer** is `kit/plumbline_verify.py`. It runs `check.py` first and requires OK; only then does it
USER enforce `x-plumbline`. It never relaxes a check: there is no switch on the command line, and the hand-time
USER allowance is read from the record, never supplied by the caller. So **`x-plumbline` is enforced by Plumbline's
USER verifier only; the standard check.py ignores it.**
USER 
USER     python3 kit/plumbline_verify.py --record R --files F --evidence E --ledger L --custody <checkout> [--json OUT]
USER 
USER The job's `custody.json` is the file `custody.json` beside `L` (the ledger, `inputs/custody-events.jsonl`).
USER 
USER | Exit | Meaning |
USER |---|---|
USER | 0 | OK at both layers. |
USER | 3 | The standard layer failed (kind `standard`; nothing else is checked). |
USER | 4 | The standard layer passed and a Plumbline check failed. |
USER | 2 | Cannot run: usage, a missing input, or a checker whose `check.py` sha256 is not the pin. |
USER 
USER Each failure prints one stdout line `FAIL <kind> <field> <file>:<line>`, and `--json OUT` writes
USER `{"exit": N, "failures": [{"kind","field","file","line"}]}`. The kinds:
USER 
USER | Kind | Enforces |
USER |---|---|
USER | `standard` | the standard `check.py` is OK (else exit 3, nothing else checked). |
USER | `schema` | every `x-plumbline` block validates against `schemas/x-plumbline/x-plumbline-v1.schema.json`, and `x-plumbline.schema` is `plumbline/x-plumbline/v1`. |
USER | `allowance` | `allowances.hand_times` is present whenever the hand-time set is non-empty, with the switch, the emitter's `writer`, and its tool-clock `at`. |
USER | `hand-time-unflagged` | a hand time the verifier recomputes from the ledger, `inputs/custody.json`, or a server-time log call is missing from `x-plumbline.hand_times[]`. |
USER | `flag-without-source` | a flag in `x-plumbline.hand_times[]` has no hand line behind it. |
USER | `events` | `generations[]`, `evaluations[]`, `relays[]` and `amendments[]` match the ledger one-to-one, each generation has a Maker call between its open and close, and each evaluation's verdict sha256 matches the file. |
USER | `relay-as-approval` | a relay is listed among approvals (a relay is a report, not an approval). |
USER | `spend` | `x-plumbline.spend` adds up: `calls_sum_usd` equals the sum of the harness logs' per-call `total_cost` within **US$0.0001** (rounding only), `total_usd = max(calls_sum_usd, key_delta_usd)`, and the key reading is stamped and read ≥ 3 min after the last settle. |
USER | `shadow` | input data appears only under `x-plumbline.inputs`; every emitter-owned key holds the value the verifier re-derives from the record, ledger and logs. |
USER 
USER A run that changes one of its inputs also fails (`writes`). The verifier never writes its inputs (V7), and it does
USER not import the emitter (V7), so its check is separate code.
USER 
USER ## Where each fact about time, endpoint, approver and revision comes from
USER 
USER A time with no tool-written ledger line or logger time behind it is a **hand time**: refused by default
USER (`hand-time`), and with `--allow-hand-times` flagged in `x-plumbline.hand_times[]` and never verified (see "Hand
USER times" above). A logged time is still cross-checked against any typed value within 60 s. A typed host or approver
USER is refused when it differs from what the log or the ledger says.
USER 
USER | Fact | Source | A hand-entered value (a typed time with no tool stamp behind it) is… |
USER |---|---|---|
USER | `startedAt` | the maker harness log: the earliest call's logger `sent_at`/`received_at` | a hand time (`inputs-no-ledger`): refused by default; under `--allow-hand-times` flagged and never verified. Cross-checked against the log within 60 s |
USER | `producedAt` | the maker harness log: the last call's logger `received_at`/`sent_at` | a hand time, as for `startedAt` |
USER | `passes.<id>.at` (`checks[].at`) | the evaluator harness call linked to the pass (`passes.<id>.call`): its logger `received_at` | a hand time even when a call is linked (`inputs-no-ledger`): refused by default, flagged under the switch, never verified; cross-checked within 60 s |
USER | control times (`controls[<id>].at`) | the linked evaluator call (`controls[].call`, default `eval_<id>` e.g. `eval_k1`): its logger `received_at` | a hand time even when a call is linked (`inputs-no-ledger`): refused by default, flagged under the switch, never verified; cross-checked within 60 s |
USER | `model.endpoint` (maker, evaluator), `checks[].endpoint` | a call made straight to a provider: the request URL host in the harness log. A call through OpenRouter (`openrouter.ai`): the upstream host **the Orchestrator declared for that seat** with `custody_ledger.py pin` before the seat's first call, and only when every call's log shows that provider served it (see "Provider pin and routing request") | refused if it differs. Never a default, never looked up from a table, never the model vendor's host by assumption |
USER | the aggregator, upstream and routing | recorded under `x-plumbline` on the maker and each evaluator: `harness_log`, `calls`, `aggregator` (`openrouter.ai`), `upstream_provider` (as the log names it), `provider_slug`, `endpoint_basis`, and `routing = {pin, requests: [{provider: <what the request body asked>, calls}], request_ok}` | — |
USER | `bar.frozenAt`, `approvals[].at`, `disposition.at` | no harness log exists: **logged live** with `kit/custody_ledger.py` (below). Each approval records `x-plumbline.time_source` | a typed value with no ledger line behind it (`inputs-no-ledger`), a ledger line with `writer: "hand"` (`ledger-hand`) or no `writer` (`ledger-no-writer`) is a hand time: refused by default, flagged under the switch, never verified |
USER | emitter revision | `git rev-parse HEAD` and `HEAD^{tree}` of the checkout the emitter runs from: `x-plumbline.emitter = {commit, tree, dirty: false, statedRevision, kit_revision}` | refused when dirty, or not the stated revision |
USER 
USER A harness-log call whose log carries no logger `sent_at`/`received_at` — only the response `Date` header or
USER `created` — is a hand time too, with source `server-header` or `response-created`. The emitter reads the server
USER time to show the value; it never writes it back into the log.
USER 
USER **Tolerance: 60 seconds** (`TOLERANCE_S` in `kit/custody_history.py`). It absorbs a time typed to the minute
USER (truncation loses up to 59 s) and ordinary clock skew between the harness host and the provider. It does not absorb
USER a time typed from memory minutes later, which is what it exists to catch. A hand-entered time may never be later
USER than the emission at all; a log-derived time may be up to 60 s later (server clock skew).
USER 
USER **Harness log format.** One JSON object per model call, a JSON list of them, or JSON Lines. Each call:
USER `{"call": "<name>", "request": {"url", "model", "messages", "provider": {…}, …}, "response": {"id", "created",
USER "model", "provider"?, "openrouter_metadata"?, …}, "response_headers": {"Date": "<RFC 7231>", …},
USER "generation"?: <the GET /api/v1/generation?id=<response id> reply>}`. The request may instead be logged as
USER `"request": {"url", "body": {…}}`. For OpenRouter calls the request **body** must be logged whole, because the
USER routing request is read from it. `call` names are needed only in a log holding several calls.
USER Each call's `model` must equal the recorded `identifier`, or end with `/<identifier>` (e.g. `deepseek/deepseek-chat`
USER for `deepseek-chat`).
USER 
USER ## Hand times: refused by default, flagged under the switch
USER 
USER A **hand time** is a time whose only source is a ledger line with `writer: "hand"` (`ledger-hand`), a ledger line
USER with no `writer` (`ledger-no-writer`), a value typed into `inputs/custody.json` with no ledger line behind it
USER (`inputs-no-ledger`), or a server time (a log time taken from the response `Date` header, `server-header`, or from
USER `created`, `response-created`) that no logger `sent_at`/`received_at` recorded.
USER 
USER **Hand times are refused by default.** With no switch, the emitter exits 3 with kind `hand-time`, prints one line
USER per hand time — `  [hand-time] <field> <file>:<line> (<source>)` — names every hand-time field and its file:line,
USER and writes nothing.
USER 
USER **The switch flags hand times and never verifies them.** `--allow-hand-times` does not check the time against a
USER log or a clock. It records each hand time in `x-plumbline.hand_times[]` and, when the set is non-empty, writes
USER `x-plumbline.allowances.hand_times = {switch: "--allow-hand-times", writer: "kit/emit_custody.py@<sha12>", at}`.
USER The listing is not disclosure-only: the verifier fails a record whose flags lack the allowance or disagree with
USER the ledger and logs (`allowance`, `hand-time-unflagged`, `flag-without-source`), and close refuses on that failure
USER (V8). The switch silences nothing else — a repo overlay whose log contradicts the hand time still exits 3 with the
USER switch on.
USER 
USER **Reported times are accepted only through the bounding commands.** A time a person reported (the Owner's
USER decision, a relay's source time, an amendment's time) is not a hand time when it is entered through a ledger
USER command that bounds it and names its source: `dispose --decided-at T --decided-source S` (bounded between the last
USER logged check and the tool's clock), `relay --source-at T` and `amendment --said-at T` (bounded between the freeze
USER and the tool's clock). The tool stamps the line and stores the reported time beside it.
USER 
USER **A relay is a report, not an approval.** The emitter never counts a relay as an approval; a buyer approval that
USER exists only as a relay is refused as a missing approval (`approval`), and the verifier reports `relay-as-approval`
USER if a relay is listed among approvals.
USER 
USER The field names used in the refusal lines, in `x-plumbline.hand_times[].field`, and by the verifier:
USER `bar.frozenAt`, `bar.approvals[<i>].at`, `routing.<seat>.declaredAt`, `disposition.at`, `startedAt`,
USER `producedAt`, `passes.<id>.at`, `controls[<id>].at`, `<log file name>#<call or index>.sent_at` and `.received_at`.
USER When a later ledger line sets the same field as an earlier one, the later line's value, line and source are the
USER ones used and reported.
