USER ## Where a program's Plumbline content lives — `dot-plumbline/`
USER 
USER Every program Plumbline touches carries its content in **`dot-plumbline/`**, in the program's own space:
USER `dot-plumbline/` in a git repo — **committed, never gitignored** (it is evidence) — and `dot-plumbline/` in a
USER Drive project folder for a program with no checkout. Spelled out, no leading dot, so it is visible in `ls`,
USER in a git host's file list, and in a Drive client, with one name in both places.
USER 
USER **Records travel with the artifact (Contracts §10.1).** Failure F6 is *"accepted work cannot be reconstructed
USER from its records"*, so a conformance audit **of wsww** lives in wsww's repo. This repo's `dot-plumbline/` holds
USER Plumbline's audits **of itself**, and the per-program split is what keeps one home per record — a second copy
USER is the drift trap the corpus names.
USER 
USER ```
USER dot-plumbline/
USER   README.md          the convention, and which doctrine commit this program obeys
USER   constitution.json  the standing frame — ratified once, versioned  [instance layer]
USER   jobs/<job_id>/     packet.json · each iteration's artifact + §5 record + per-dimension vectors
USER                      · instrument/  the rubric AND runner that produced the records, content-addressed
USER   run-log.json       append-only: run id · commit · seats · bounds · dimensions + verdict · escalation · cost
USER   audits/            conformance audits of THIS program — §5 records, or a labelled readings record
USER   probes/            measurements taken on this program — the reading plus the protocol that produced
USER                      it (operator burden, the JEV evaluator probe)
USER   operating-loop.jsonl  append-only history of the operating loop: one line per run, written by
USER                      tools/check_operating_loop.py — the commit, the checks that ran, the failures
USER ```
USER 
USER Only what exists is present. `jobs/` and `run-log.json` appeared when job one ran; an empty placeholder is
USER structure nothing asked for.
USER 
USER **Retain the instrument, not just its hash.** The record names the runner's sha256 (C30), but the runner is a
USER source file that keeps changing — so the instrument that produced a past record is gone unless it was filed.
USER On job one the runner was patched after the run and the census's instrument no longer existed on disk; it had
USER to be reconstructed from git history. The runner now **archives itself content-addressed** on every run
USER (`instrument/run_conformance_read.<sha12>.py`), so a later version never overwrites an earlier one and two
USER instruments can coexist in one job.
