USER ## Who may approve
USER 
USER custody approvals are records of who approved, not signatures. The emitter keeps them that way and refuses an
USER approval that misstates who approved:
USER 
USER - The ledger must name the orchestrating agent: `"orchestrator": {"name": "VizH (Chief of Staff)", "kind": "agent", "aliases": ["VizH"]}`. Without it, every approval is refused.
USER - An approval by the orchestrator (name or alias, case-insensitive) is refused, **always**, even marked as an agent.
USER - An approval by a model (`kind: "model"`, or `by` naming the maker or an evaluator, by name, id or identifier) is refused.
USER - `kind` is `person` (default), `agent` or `model`. An `agent` approval counts only with `adopted: {document, version}` set (the agent adopted a published document). A `person` approval whose `by` reads like an agent (agent, bot, assistant, orchestrator, chief of staff, gpt, claude, grok, …) is refused: mark it, or have the person approve.
USER - If the packet names a `buyer` or `specialist`, the approval for that role must be by the same party.
USER - **A relay is a report, not an approval.** An approval that exists only as a relay is refused as a missing approval; the emitter never promotes a relay to an approver.
USER 
USER ## The emitter's revision
USER 
USER The record names the commit and tree of the checkout the emitter ran from. The emitter refuses (exit 3) when:
USER 
USER - it is not running from a git checkout;
USER - the checkout has a tracked change, or an untracked file that is not ignored, **outside `dot-plumbline/` and the
USER   job folder being emitted** (both are job state, not code);
USER - a stated revision is given (`--expect-revision`, `$PLUMBLINE_EXPECT_REVISION`, or ledger `emitter_revision`) and
USER   it is a prefix (≥ 7 hex) of neither the commit nor the tree. **Use the tree**: `git am` re-creates the commit with
USER   a new SHA, but the tree is the same bytes.
