USER """What a custody record may say about its own history, and where each fact must come from.
USER 
USER kit/emit_custody.py writes a custody/v1 record from a job's files. Iteration 1 trusted the Orchestrator's ledger
USER (inputs/custody.json) for times, endpoints and approvers. Real runs showed what that costs: round-minute times
USER typed after the fact (some later than the record itself), the model vendor's host recorded when every call went
USER through a router to someone else, and the orchestrating agent recorded as the Specialist. A checker cannot see
USER any of that, so the emitter must refuse to write it. This module holds those refusals. Stdlib only.
USER 
USER   TIMES      Every model call's time comes from its harness log: the logger's own `sent_at` / `received_at` when
USER              the log carries them, else the HTTP `Date` response header (server-set), else the response's
USER              `created` epoch. A hand-entered time for the same event is kept only as a cross-check: if it differs
USER              from the log by more than TOLERANCE_S the emitter refuses. Event order (frozenAt < startedAt <=
USER              producedAt <= checks <= close; frozenAt <= approvals <= startedAt; disposition >= the last check)
USER              and "nothing dated after the emit time" are refusals too.
USER   SEND/RECV  The harness log also carries the logger's own `sent_at` (when the request was sent) and
USER              `received_at` (when the reply came back). The maker's `startedAt` is the FIRST call's `sent_at`
USER              and `producedAt` is the LAST call's `received_at`; a check's `at` is its call's `received_at`.
USER              Each such time carries an `x-plumbline` source naming the log file and the line that carries it.
USER              A call with no logger `sent_at`/`received_at` is a hand time: its time would come from the
USER              response `Date` header (`server-header`) or the response `created` epoch (`response-created`).
USER              The emitter never writes it back to the log; it refuses it by default, and under
USER              `--allow-hand-times` lists it in `x-plumbline.hand_times[]`. `Call.hand_time_entries()` reports
USER              those entries.
