USER ## Models
USER 
USER custody CL1 needs at least one model evaluator per batch that is not the maker's model:
USER 
USER - `sameModelAsMaker` is computed from the identifiers, and true is a separation gap.
USER - A different **family** keeps `separationGaps` empty. The emitter adds an entry automatically when the families match.
USER 
USER A small, cheap setup is enough:
USER 
USER | Seat | Example | Notes |
USER |---|---|---|
USER | Maker | any model, `kind: "model"`, e.g. family `deepseek` | Writes the artifact. Record `{family, identifier, readOnly: false}`; the endpoint comes from its harness log. Save the log as `custody/evidence/harness-maker*.log`. |
USER | Evaluator | a different family, e.g. `glm`, read-only | One call per pass and per control, each named in the log (`eval_artifact`, `eval_k1`, `eval_k2`). Save its raw reply as the evidence file, and save the harness log (CL1-15). |
USER 
USER Use `family` lowercase with hyphens and no version (`^[a-z]+(-[a-z]+)*$`). **Endpoint**: call the provider
USER directly (the request URL host is then the endpoint), or through OpenRouter with the seat **pinned** to one
USER upstream provider, declared before the seat starts (next section).
USER 
USER ## Provider pin and routing request (OpenRouter)
USER 
USER custody SPEC section 4: the endpoint is the service that answered, and a router is not it. Through OpenRouter, the
USER emitter therefore needs (1) which provider the Orchestrator chose, declared before any call, (2) proof in each
USER call's log that this provider, and no other, served it, and (3) the request having asked OpenRouter for exactly
USER that. The emitter guesses none of these.
USER 
USER **1. Declare the pin** for each seat before the seat's first call (the Maker's pin before the Maker starts;
USER declare every seat's pin then, so no seat is chosen after seeing output):
USER 
USER ```sh
USER python3 kit/custody_ledger.py pin --job-dir J --seat maker  --provider "<name as OpenRouter's responses give it>" \
USER     --slug <provider slug for provider.order> --host <that provider's own API host>
USER python3 kit/custody_ledger.py pin --job-dir J --seat eval-a --provider "…" --slug … --host …
USER ```
USER 
USER It writes `routing.<seat> = {via: "openrouter.ai", provider, slug, host, declaredAt}` into `inputs/custody.json`
USER and a `pin` event into `inputs/custody-events.jsonl`. It refuses (exit 3) once the seat's harness log records a
USER call, and refuses (exit 2) the aggregator's own host as `--host`. `--host` is the host the record will name as
USER the endpoint. It is the Orchestrator's declaration, recorded as such in `endpoint_basis`. The emitter does not
USER look it up.
USER 
USER **2. Who served each call** is read from the log, in this order. All are OpenRouter-documented fields:
USER 
USER | Source in the call's log | OpenRouter documentation |
USER |---|---|
USER | `response.provider` | the provider name on a sync response. `openapi.json` refers to "the `provider` field of sync responses" (BatchObject.provider). It is **not** listed in the ChatResult schema at https://openrouter.ai/docs/api/reference/overview |
USER | `response.openrouter_metadata.endpoints.available[selected].provider`, `…attempts[].provider` | opt-in: send header `X-OpenRouter-Metadata: enabled` (https://openrouter.ai/openapi.json, `/chat/completions` parameters; schemas OpenRouterMetadata, RouterAttempt) |
USER | `generation.data.provider_name`, `generation.data.provider_responses[]` | `GET https://openrouter.ai/api/v1/generation?id=<response id>` (https://openrouter.ai/docs/api/api-reference/generations/get-generation). The harness logs this reply under `generation` |
USER 
USER Response headers are not read. OpenRouter documents no provider header. A name matches the pin when it equals,
USER ignoring case and punctuation, the pinned `provider` or the first part of the pinned `slug` (`deepinfra/fp8` →
USER `deepinfra`). **Log `generation` or enable `openrouter_metadata`**. These are the only sources that show the
USER providers *tried*, so they are the only way to see a fallback that failed over.
USER 
USER **3. The routing request** is read from each call's request body, `provider` object
USER (https://openrouter.ai/docs/features/provider-routing):
USER 
USER | Field | Required value | Why |
USER |---|---|---|
USER | `provider.order` | exactly one slug, equal to the pinned `slug` | "OpenRouter will try them one at a time and proceed to other providers if none are operational" |
USER | `provider.allow_fallbacks` | `false` | default `true`; the doc: "If you don't want to allow any other providers, you should disable fallbacks as well" |
USER | `provider.zdr` | `true` (preferred), **or** `provider.data_collection: "deny"` | `zdr: true` restricts routing to Zero Data Retention endpoints. `data_collection: "deny"` only excludes providers that store or train on data, which is weaker. The doc says the request's `zdr` is ORed with the account setting, so it can only turn ZDR on |
USER | `models` | absent or a single model | a list of several is a model fallback |
USER 
USER **Refuse or gap: a request that did not ask for this is a HARD GAP (exit 1), not a refusal.** The record is
USER written, with `x-plumbline.gaps[]` field `routing.<seat>.request` naming what was missing, and
USER `x-plumbline.routing.request_ok: false` next to exactly what was asked. Why a gap:
USER 
USER - A refusal (exit 3) in this emitter means the record *would misstate* a fact. Here every fact it writes is still
USER   true. The endpoint is backed by the log's served-by check, and the request is recorded verbatim.
USER - What is wrong is the run, not the record, and re-emitting cannot fix it. A refusal would withhold the only
USER   document that shows a stranger the prompt was not held to one zero-retention provider. A hard gap keeps the
USER   record honest and visible, and it still exits non-zero. A missing request body is the same hard gap ("cannot
USER   be read").
USER - What *would* misstate is still refused: a fallback that happened, a served-by the log does not show, or a
USER   late pin.
USER 
USER The exact lines (fixtures `tests/fixtures/custody/failures/pin-*`, `routing-unpinned`):
USER 
USER ```
USER # no pin declared for a seat that went through OpenRouter (exit 3)
USER [endpoint] maker: harness-maker-fixture.log#0 went to the aggregator openrouter.ai and no provider pin was declared for this seat (custody_ledger.py pin, before its first call); the emitter will not guess which host answered (custody SPEC 4: a router is not the service that answered)
USER # fallback: another provider served (pin-fallback, exit 3)
USER [endpoint] maker: harness-maker-fixture.log#0 was served by 'Together' (response provider), 'Together' (generation provider_name), not the pinned provider 'DeepInfra' (deepinfra/fp8): a fallback to another provider; the record would name api.deepinfra.com for a call it did not answer
USER # provider missing from the log (pin-provider-missing, exit 3)
USER [endpoint] maker: harness-maker-fixture.log#0 went to openrouter.ai and the log does not say which provider served it (no response `provider`, no `openrouter_metadata`, no logged /api/v1/generation stats); the pin to 'DeepInfra' cannot be confirmed and the emitter will not guess
USER # pin after the first call (pin-late, exit 3)
USER [order] maker: provider pin declared at '2026-10-20T11:05:00Z', after the seat's first call harness-maker-fixture.log#0 (2026-10-20T11:00:00Z); a pin chosen after the calls is not a pin
USER # routing request without one provider / no-fallback / ZDR (routing-unpinned, exit 1, record written)
USER GAP  routing.maker.request: harness-maker-fixture.log#0 did not ask OpenRouter for the pin: provider.order is ['deepinfra/fp8', 'together'], not exactly one provider; provider.allow_fallbacks is absent, not false (OpenRouter's default is true: other providers may serve); neither provider.zdr true nor provider.data_collection "deny" (zdr absent, data_collection absent). The served provider is still read from the log, but nothing in the request kept the prompt on one zero-retention provider. Re-emitting cannot fix this; the run's own request did it.
USER # request body not logged (exit 1): the same gap, reason "the log does not carry the request body, so the routing request cannot be read"
USER ```
USER 
USER **Model slugs** (OpenRouter `GET https://openrouter.ai/api/v1/models`, read 2026-10-27):
USER 
USER | Seat | Model | Slug | Note |
USER |---|---|---|---|
USER | Maker | DeepSeek V4.1 Flash 1017 | `deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash-1017` | canonical `deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash-20261017`. Not `deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash` (the 0910 release) |
