USER #!/usr/bin/env python3
USER """Plumbline behaviour suite.
USER 
USER Two jobs, and they are different jobs:
USER 
USER   PART 1 — DOCTRINE. Each case is a rule from the seven weight-bearing files, with a
USER   packet or record that must pass and one that must fail. A case that cannot fail is not
USER   a check (Instrument rule 3: a check that cannot fail certifies nothing).
USER 
USER   PART 2 — ALIGNMENT (the reason this suite exists in this form). Plumbline's schemas are
USER   DERIVED from the corpus. The invariant that makes that real is one-directional:
USER 
USER       every packet Plumbline accepts, the corpus must accept too.
USER 
USER   Plumbline is an instance (Contracts §8): it may constrain itself MORE, never less. If its
USER   schema ever accepts something the corpus rejects, Plumbline has quietly become its own law.
USER   So the corpus's own fixture battery is run through both validators and the direction is
USER   asserted, not assumed.
USER 
USER   PART 3 — CUSTODY. custody/v1 records (kit/emit_custody.py) are checked against a schema
USER   DERIVED from the pinned evaluatordriven/custody schema. These cases make the custody drift
USER   check able to fail (a moved custody, a hand-edited derived schema) and pin the emitter's
USER   refusals. They need no corpus: set CUSTODY_DIR to a custody checkout to test a real move.
USER 
USER   The fixture battery and its direction invariant run on the VENDORED copy of the corpus's schemas and
USER   fixtures (schemas/vendor/e-di@<pin8>/), so they run everywhere. Two checks need the corpus itself and are
USER   reported by id (tools/edi_corpus.py line forms): corpus.pin-on-disk (the pin still matches the corpus on
USER   disk) and corpus.battery-equality (the vendored copy is still the corpus's bytes). With no corpus each
USER   prints a SKIP[corpus] line; with PLUMBLINE_REQUIRE_CORPUS=1 each is a FAIL[corpus] line and exit 1.
